---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import { SectionHeading } from '../../components/SectionHeading';
import { Prose } from '../../components/Prose';
import { Metrics } from '../../components/Metrics';
import { Explorations } from '../../components/Explorations';
import { Hindsight } from '../../components/Hindsight';
import { Contribution } from '../../components/Contribution';
import { Media } from '../../components/Media';

/**
 * DRAFT. Study one of two: the Design Systems discipline.
 *
 * Every fact here came from Rohit directly, from the Design System Summary
 * FigJam, or from the EGDS Components and Theming file. Nothing is invented,
 * and no metric has been added that does not exist. The `[NEEDS: …]` markers
 * are rendered visibly because a gap you can see in a browser gets closed.
 *
 * The decision quotes in the explorations are verbatim from a plan.md
 * screenshot on the FigJam. Confirm the component they belong to before this
 * ships: the screenshot was legible but not labelled.
 */
---

<CaseStudy
  title="A component library a machine could read"
  slug="machine-readable-components"
  standfirst="Expedia Group's components had drifted across iOS, Android and web. We rebuilt them against a single written specification that designers, engineers and language models could all build from."
>
  <Contribution
    slot="contribution"
    items={[
      { term: 'Role', detail: 'Product Designer in the New Component Architecture pod, Expedia Group Design System' },
      { term: 'What I owned', detail: 'Component ownership end to end: reading the generated plan, rebuilding the component from the ground up in Figma, and delivering the specification engineering built from. I also created the designer onboarding for the pod as it grew.' },
      { term: 'The team', detail: 'Three designers and a principal, with Nathan Curtis contracted in for guidance and to pick up remaining components. Engineering implemented from the spec.' },
      { term: 'Duration', detail: '2025, with user testing in Q1 2026' }
    ]}
  />

  <div slot="problem">
    <SectionHeading standfirst="Three platforms had implemented the same components independently for years. Nobody knew how far apart they had drifted until we asked a machine to read all three.">
      The problem
    </SectionHeading>
    <Prose lead>
      <p>
        A component in the design system was really three components: one in the
        iOS repo, one in Android, one in web. Each had its own props, its own
        arrangement rules, its own accumulated exceptions. The Figma library was
        a fourth version that matched none of them exactly.
      </p>
      <p>
        The team's answer was to stop treating any implementation as the source
        of truth. A single written specification would define the component,
        and Figma, all three platforms and eventually a language model would
        build from that one document.
      </p>
      <p>
        [NEEDS: the insight, and it is recoverable. Scraping three repos for one
        component produced a plan that showed exactly how far apart they were.
        How many props existed on one platform and not the others? How many
        components turned out to be genuinely different rather than accidentally
        different? That number is both the problem statement and the baseline
        the work should be measured against, and it is sitting in the plan files
        in GitHub.]
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </div>

  <div slot="process">
    <SectionHeading standfirst="A machine proposed each component by reading the code. A person decided what was actually right.">
      How a component got rebuilt
    </SectionHeading>
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Every component started as a scrape. We read the iOS, Android and web
        repositories for that component and generated a plan, which lived in
        GitHub next to the component itself. The plan carried recommendations,
        configurations, elements, decisions, open questions and TODOs.
      </p>
      <p>
        The plan was a proposal, not an instruction. I rebuilt each component
        from the ground up against it: variables bound, slots attached, auto
        layout throughout, naming corrected, hidden and code-only properties
        declared, accessibility labels specified. Where the plan was wrong, I
        challenged it and recorded why. Once the component and its specification
        were finished, engineering implemented from the spec, and Code Connect
        established a one to one match between Figma and code afterwards.
      </p>
      <p>
        [NEEDS: one concrete example of the workflow changing because of what
        Q1 2026 testing showed. It was run with Expedia product designers, and
        with product managers and engineers who opted in. A reader asks
        specifically for a trade-off made because of what testing told you, and
        that is currently the weakest part of this study.]
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <SectionHeading level={3}>What the plan proposed, and what we overruled</SectionHeading>
    <Prose>
      <p>
        The rejected paths are unusually well evidenced here, because the
        alternative was not imagined. It was proposed by a machine reading three
        real codebases, and then overruled by a person who could say why.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <Explorations
      items={[
        {
          title: 'A vertical offset property, because React already had one',
          why: 'Rejected. Parents control arrangement and spacing outside a component, not the component itself. Carrying the prop forward would have moved a layout decision inside a boundary it does not belong in, on every platform, permanently.',
          image: { alt: 'The generated plan showing the vertical offset decision and its rationale' }
        },
        {
          title: 'A dark frame variant for inverse surfaces',
          why: 'Rejected in favour of styling with inverse tokens. A forced dark frame hardcodes a theme into a component; tokens let the surface decide, which is what made the system themable across brands.',
          image: { alt: 'The plan entry recommending inverse tokens over a dark frame' }
        },
        {
          title: 'A maximum height, so message content crops after two lines',
          why: 'Left open, then rejected as a built-in constraint. The recommendation was that cropping is guidance rather than something the component enforces. Building it in would have made the component wrong for every case that legitimately needed three lines.',
          image: { alt: 'The open question about message cropping and the recommendation against a max height' }
        }
      ]}
    />
  </div>

  <div slot="interface">
    <SectionHeading standfirst="One component, three platforms, each honouring its own conventions rather than one design relabelled three times.">
      What shipped
    </SectionHeading>
    <Media
      alt="The EGDS Dialog component set showing Web, iOS and Android variants of the same component"
      ratio="4-3"
      caption="Platform is a variant axis on a single component. The iOS variant uses native alert conventions, a centred heading and a text action above a hairline, rather than inheriting the web treatment."
    />
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Dialog is a fair example of the shape. Platform sits as a variant axis on
        one component set rather than as three separate components. Its actions
        are a nested set covering one to three actions, primary or tertiary
        emphasis, and horizontal or vertical arrangement, built again for iOS
        where the conventions differ.
      </p>
      <p>
        Slots arrived in beta partway through, and we had already built
        components with placeholder slots in anticipation, so adopting them was
        a substitution rather than a rebuild.
      </p>
      <p>
        [NEEDS: a second example, ideally one that was harder than Dialog.
        Dialog is clean. The reviewer is looking for complexity untangled, so
        the component that fought back is the better story.]
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </div>

  <div slot="impact">
    <SectionHeading standfirst="The pod was made redundant before the system could be measured in production. What follows is what was true when the work stopped.">
      Impact
    </SectionHeading>
    <Metrics
      items={[
        { value: '[NEEDS]', label: 'components rebuilt against a single specification' },
        { value: '3', label: 'platform implementations collapsed into one source of truth, from iOS, Android and web' },
        { value: '90+', label: 'brands the system serves' }
      ]}
      source="[NEEDS: the component count, and confirmation that 90+ brands is a figure you can cite from a system source rather than from a colleague's LinkedIn. The proof of concept demonstrated a shorter path from idea to code; if anyone timed it even once, that figure belongs here and would carry this section on its own.]"
    />
    <Prose>
      <p>
        [NEEDS: this section is the one a reader gates hardest on, and it is
        currently the weakest. Recoverable candidates, in order of strength:
        anything measured during the Q1 2026 testing; the time to produce a
        component specification before and after the scraping workflow, which
        the tracking board's dates would show; and the divergence the scrape
        found between platforms, which is a real number sitting in the plans.]
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </div>

  <div slot="hindsight">
    <Hindsight>
      <p>
        We never instrumented it. The pod was made redundant before the
        architecture reached production, so the claim the whole project rested
        on, that the distance from idea to shipped code gets shorter, has a
        proof of concept behind it and no measurement.
      </p>
      <p>
        That was avoidable and it was my blind spot as much as anyone's. The
        baseline was measurable from the first scrape: we knew how long a
        component took to specify the old way, and we could have recorded it.
        Instrumenting at the start costs a day. Reconstructing it afterwards, as
        I am finding while writing this, is not possible at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        [NEEDS: your own read. This is written from what you told me and it
        should sound like you. A reader calls out self-awareness explicitly, so
        this section is worth more than its length suggests. Keep it factual and
        free of grievance.]
      </p>
    </Hindsight>
  </div>
</CaseStudy>
